# Frozen SolidWorks documents

After a document reaches a user-approved locked-in state, **stop all CAD mutation** on that document until the user explicitly asks to edit it again.

## When a doc becomes frozen

A document is frozen when **all** of the following are true:

1. The user approved the result ("looks good" / equivalent).
2. `solidworks_confirm_and_save` returned `ok: true` (and `poseStable: true` for assemblies).
3. You told the user it is locked in.

Also treat as frozen if the user says freeze / leave it / don't touch it / hands off for a named document.

## While frozen — allowed

- Read-only inspect: `list_*`, `status`, `measure`, `export` PNG (read-only view capture), `diagnose_*`, `search_*`
- Answering questions about the frozen doc from inspection or prior context

## While frozen — forbidden (unless user explicitly re-opens work)

- Any mate create/edit/delete/suppress
- `set_mate_limit_angle`, transforms, dimensions, features, rebuilds meant to "heal"
- `save_document`, `confirm_and_save`, checkpoint restore, replace/insert/delete components
- "Just one more force rebuild / park / flip / recreate" loops
- Opening a healing script against the frozen path

If save/inspect shows mate errors on a frozen doc, **report them and wait**. Do not auto-heal.

## Unfreeze

Only when the user clearly asks to change that document again (e.g. "fix the roll limit", "edit the arm asm", "unfreeze marengo_arm_right_asm"). Then resume the normal confirm-and-save workflow.

## Session hygiene

- Prefer one locked-in assembly per conversation turn; after lock-in, switch to read-only or stop.
- Do not keep probing/healing the same joint across turns "to be safe".
- Scratch `.tmp-*` heal scripts must not target a frozen doc.
